Z||000000||8Ball||09-22-2001||11:05 AM||||Ok as some of you know I have an 86 2m6 that I have been working on getting road ready for a couple of months now. The coolant system keeps boiling over out of the coolant bottle. So far I have replaced the water pump the rad car and the thermo cap and all the hoses. I have a coolant fan on the way I think. And i just bought a new thermostat but have not had a chance to put it in.<P> But after I put on the new caps I wanted to start it and see if that helped any. When I did white smoke came out of the pipes for about 1 and a half minutes then it went away. And I am hearing a hissing from the engine like steam escapeing. I have a blown head gasket don't I?<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> Say it ain't so  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||09-22-2001||01:56 PM||||How fast is the coolant blowing out after startup? <P>Disconnect the OE recovery tank and connect a longer hose so you can reach a 5 gallon pail. (This is mainly so you stop dumping coolant on the ground...) Be carefull you don't get scalded. Use a meat thermometer and measure the coolant comming out. If the coolant is being pushed out at low temperatures then you probably have a head problem.<P>Run a compression test on it. That may tell you which side is screwed up. <P>This can also be checked with a gas analyzer commonly used for emissions work... if the analyzer senses exhaust fumes exiting the system then it's definatly got a problem.||||reg||1
Z||000002||8Ball||09-22-2001||02:28 PM||||Well it usually starts after about 15 miles of driving. And it comes out HOT!!! really hot. Now I absolutly have to get the car on the road as my daily driver (A.K.A. Northern Rusty bottom) has just had the lower a arm break in half   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> So it is out of commision for a while til I can afford to have it fixed. I am going to put my new thermostat in the Fiero right now Then I will drive it some to see what happens.<P>Thanks,<BR>Randy<P><BR>Well I don't think it is boiling anymore. I think the thermostat fixed that. But now (of coarse) it is spitting water out from the gasket are of the new water pump. I must have done something wront. That is also the hissing sound I was hearing. So both our cars are going into the shop monday. Now I just have to borrow a car so I can work this week. An on-site PC-Tech can't go on-site without a car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by 8Ball (edited 09-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||87GTZ34||09-23-2001||11:56 AM||||8Ball,<P>  I wanted to pass along a homemade tool idea that I use and (for the Fiero's cooling system) has turned out to be invaluable.  I bought a spare cap and drilled a hole 7/16 i think and put a car tubeless tire valve in it.  When I want to check the cooling system for leaks I like to do while the car is cool for obvious reasons.  I put this cap on a cold car and use a hand pump and pump it until I hear the oveflow system/tank burp.  At that point if your radiator cap is good you have 15lbs pressure in a cold system.  I used a tire pressure gauge to verify the 15lbs and bingo.  I then found all of my leaks including slow seeping leaks around brand new hoses that where not tight enough or needed cleaning on metal that they attached to.  You should be able to use this method to find all of your leaks.  GL!<P>PS my 1st time posting pics so be patient.<P>pressure test coolant leaks tools homemade overflow radiator waterpump thermostat overheat<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Capprestesttop.JPG">   <P><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/capprestestbtm.JPG">   <P>edited to add search keywords. hah posting pictures worked the 1st time<BR><p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 09-23-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Leadfoot||09-23-2001||01:46 PM||||Does the fan kick on?  Also, check your spark plugs.  If you find one that is super clean then you most likely have a bad head gasket.<p>[This message has been edited by Leadfoot (edited 09-23-2001).]||||reg||1
